WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.874
A fraction counts equal-sized pieces of one whole

00:00:03.874 --> 00:00:09.954
Splitting each third into four smaller pieces creates twelfths without changing the shaded amount

00:00:09.954 --> 00:00:15.600
One third and four twelfths therefore describe the same quantity in different units.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.929
To add fractions, first give their pieces a common size

00:00:20.929 --> 00:00:25.362
One third contributes four twelfths and one fourth contributes three twelfths

00:00:25.362 --> 00:00:30.600
We add the piece counts, while twelve remains the name of the unit.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:35.230
Multiplication asks for a fraction of another amount

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:39.808
Two thirds of three fourths covers six of twelve equal cells

00:00:39.808 --> 00:00:47.300
Here we multiply the fractions; we are not combining two separate amounts, so a common denominator is unnecessary.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.421
Division asks how many divisor-sized units fit

00:00:51.421 --> 00:00:56.659
A length of one and a half contains two lengths of three fourths

00:00:56.659 --> 00:01:02.300
Multiplying by the reciprocal gives the same count, provided the divisor is not zero.
